0..55 Comment "// What is open crosses back from a migration as bytes."
56..58 Comment "//"
59..134 Comment "// A crossing asks two judgements. Mobility is authority: nothing the value"
135..209 Comment "// holds is meaningful only where it was made. Openness is representation:"
210..284 Comment "// the value names no location, so its bytes read the same at every world."
285..360 Comment "// They agree on data and part at a handle, which is mobile always and open"
361..386 Comment "// when what it holds is."
387..389 Comment "//"
390..466 Comment "// Each declaration below migrates to the seat and returns its result to the"
467..540 Comment "// operator. Data of integers is open, and so is a handle to it. A closed"
541..616 Comment "// thunk is open too, since it captures nothing. A field the operator reads"
617..667 Comment "// from what comes back reads as it does in place."
668..670 Comment "//"
671..747 Comment "// - see: Rainey et al., \"LoCalMem\", PACMPL 10 (ICFP 2026), § 5.1, openness"
748..823 Comment "// - see: Murphy, Crary and Harper, \"Type-Safe Distributed Programming with"
824..849 Comment "//   ML5\", TGC 2007, § 3"
850..919 Comment "// - see: `pathological/worlds/open.gandr` — what is not open stays"
920..994 Comment "// - see: #3275 — `projectedDone`, `projectedInner` and `forcedLater`, a"
995..1044 Comment "//   field read from a record a migration answers"
1046..1112 Comment "// The operator\'s and the seat\'s worlds, each declaration\'s entry."
